{"surah": 53, "verses": [{"ayah": 1, "text": "وَٱلنَّجْمِ إِذَا هَوَىٰ ", "translation": "By the star when it descends,", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَٱلنَّجْمِ", "translit": "ِmْjgَّnlٱَw", "root": "نجم"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "إِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "هَوَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَwَh", "root": "هوي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 2, "text": "مَا ضَلَّ صَاحِبُكُمْ وَمَا غَوَىٰ ", "translation": "Your companion [i.e., Muḥammad] has not strayed, nor has he erred,", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ضَلَّ", "translit": "َّlَḍ", "root": "ضلل"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "صَاحِبُكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkُbِḥāَṣ", "root": "صحب"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "غَوَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَwَغ", "root": "غوي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 3, "text": "وَمَا يَنطِقُ عَنِ ٱلْهَوَىٰٓ ", "translation": "Nor does he speak from [his own] inclination.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَنطِقُ", "translit": "ُqِṭnَy", "root": "نطق"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "عَنِ", "translit": "ِnَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْهَوَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَwَhْlٱ", "root": "هوي"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 4, "text": "إِنْ هُوَ إِلَّا وَحْىٌ يُوحَىٰ ", "translation": "It is not but a revelation revealed,", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنْ", "translit": "ْnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "هُوَ", "translit": "َwُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَحْىٌ", "translit": "ٌàْḥَw", "root": "وحي"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "يُوحَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَḥwُy", "root": "وحي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 5, "text": "عَلَّمَهُۥ شَدِيدُ ٱلْقُوَىٰ ", "translation": "Taught to him by one intense in strength [i.e., Gabriel] -", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "عَلَّمَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَmَّlَʿع", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "شَدِيدُ", "translit": "ُdyِdَhs", "root": "شدد"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْقُوَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَwُqْlٱ", "root": "قوي"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 6, "text": "ذُو مِرَّةٍ فَٱسْتَوَىٰ ", "translation": "One of soundness.[1] And he rose to [his] true form[2]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ذُو", "translit": "wُhd", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مِرَّةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَّrِm", "root": "مرر"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فَٱسْتَوَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَwَtْsٱَf", "root": "سوي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 7, "text": "وَهُوَ بِٱلْأُفُقِ ٱلْأَعْلَىٰ ", "translation": "While he was in the higher [part of the] horizon.[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَهُوَ", "translit": "َwُhَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "بِٱلْأُفُقِ", "translit": "ِqُfُaʾْlٱِb", "root": "افق"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْأَعْلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlْʿعَaʾْlٱ", "root": "علو"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 8, "text": "ثُمَّ دَنَا فَتَدَلَّىٰ ", "translation": "Then he approached and descended", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ثُمَّ", "translit": "َّmُht", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "دَنَا", "translit": "āَnَd", "root": "دنو"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فَتَدَلَّىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَّlَdَtَf", "root": "دلو"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 9, "text": "فَكَانَ قَابَ قَوْسَيْنِ أَوْ أَدْنَىٰ ", "translation": "And was at a distance of two bow lengths or nearer.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَكَانَ", "translit": "َnāَkَf", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "قَابَ", "translit": "َbāَq", "root": "قوب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "قَوْسَيْنِ", "translit": "ِnْyَsْwَq", "root": "قوس"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أَوْ", "translit": "ْwَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "أَدْنَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَnْdَaʾ", "root": "دنو"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 10, "text": "فَأَوْحَىٰٓ إِلَىٰ عَبْدِهِۦ مَآ أَوْحَىٰ ", "translation": "And he revealed to His Servant[1] what he revealed [i.e., conveyed].", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَأَوْحَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَḥْwَaʾَf", "root": "وحي"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "إِلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "عَبْدِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِdْbَʿع", "root": "عبد"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مَآ", "translit": "ٓāَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "أَوْحَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَḥْwَaʾ", "root": "وحي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 11, "text": "مَا كَذَبَ ٱلْفُؤَادُ مَا رَأَىٰٓ ", "translation": "The heart[1] did not lie [about] what it saw.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "كَذَبَ", "translit": "َbَhdَk", "root": "كذب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْفُؤَادُ", "translit": "ُdāَwʾُfْlٱ", "root": "فاد"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "رَأَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَaʾَr", "root": "راي"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 12, "text": "أَفَتُمَٰرُونَهُۥ عَلَىٰ مَا يَرَىٰ ", "translation": "So will you dispute with him over what he saw?", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَفَتُمَٰرُونَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَnwُrَٰmُtَfَaʾ", "root": "مري"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "عَلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "يَرَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَrَy", "root": "راي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 13, "text": "وَلَقَدْ رَءَاهُ نَزْلَةً أُخْرَىٰ ", "translation": "And he certainly saw him in another descent[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَقَدْ", "translit": "ْdَqَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "رَءَاهُ", "translit": "ُhāَءَr", "root": "راي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "نَزْلَةً", "translit": "ًẗَlْzَn", "root": "نزل"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أُخْرَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَrْhkُaʾ", "root": "اخر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 14, "text": "عِندَ سِدْرَةِ ٱلْمُنتَهَىٰ ", "translation": "At the Lote Tree of the Utmost Boundary -", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "عِندَ", "translit": "َdnِʿع", "root": "عند"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "سِدْرَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَrْdِs", "root": "سدر"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْمُنتَهَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَhَtnُmْlٱ", "root": "نهي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 15, "text": "عِندَهَا جَنَّةُ ٱلْمَأْوَىٰٓ ", "translation": "Near it is the Garden of Refuge [i.e., Paradise] -", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "عِندَهَا", "translit": "āَhَdnِʿع", "root": "عند"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "جَنَّةُ", "translit": "ُẗَّnَjg", "root": "جنن"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْمَأْوَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَwْaʾَmْlٱ", "root": "اوي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 16, "text": "إِذْ يَغْشَى ٱلسِّدْرَةَ مَا يَغْشَىٰ ", "translation": "When there covered the Lote Tree that which covered [it].[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِذْ", "translit": "ْhdِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَغْشَى", "translit": "àَhsْغَy", "root": "غشو"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلسِّدْرَةَ", "translit": "َẗَrْdِّslٱ", "root": "سدر"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "يَغْشَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَhsْغَy", "root": "غشو"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 17, "text": "مَا زَاغَ ٱلْبَصَرُ وَمَا طَغَىٰ ", "translation": "The sight [of the Prophet (ﷺ)] did not swerve, nor did it transgress [its limit].", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "زَاغَ", "translit": "َغāَz", "root": "زيغ"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْبَصَرُ", "translit": "ُrَṣَbْlٱ", "root": "بصر"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "طَغَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَغَṭ", "root": "طغي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 18, "text": "لَقَدْ رَأَىٰ مِنْ ءَايَٰتِ رَبِّهِ ٱلْكُبْرَىٰٓ ", "translation": "He certainly saw of the greatest signs of his Lord.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "لَقَدْ", "translit": "ْdَqَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "رَأَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَaʾَr", "root": "راي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مِنْ", "translit": "ْnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ءَايَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰyāَء", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "رَبِّهِ", "translit": "ِhِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلْكُبْرَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَrْbُkْlٱ", "root": "كبر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 19, "text": "أَفَرَءَيْتُمُ ٱللَّٰتَ وَٱلْعُزَّىٰ ", "translation": "So have you considered al-Lāt and al-ʿUzzā?", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَفَرَءَيْتُمُ", "translit": "ُmُtْyَءَrَfَaʾ", "root": "راي"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱللَّٰتَ", "translit": "َtَّٰllٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَٱلْعُزَّىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَّzُʿعْlٱَw", "root": "عزز"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 20, "text": "وَمَنَوٰةَ ٱلثَّالِثَةَ ٱلْأُخْرَىٰٓ ", "translation": "And Manāt, the third - the other one?[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَمَنَوٰةَ", "translit": "َẗٰwَnَmَw", "root": "مني"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلثَّالِثَةَ", "translit": "َẗَhtِlāَّhtlٱ", "root": "ثلث"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْأُخْرَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَrْhkُaʾْlٱ", "root": "اخر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 21, "text": "أَلَكُمُ ٱلذَّكَرُ وَلَهُ ٱلْأُنثَىٰ ", "translation": "Is the male for you and for Him the female?", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَلَكُمُ", "translit": "ُmُkَlَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلذَّكَرُ", "translit": "ُrَkَّhdlٱ", "root": "ذكر"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَلَهُ", "translit": "ُhَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْأُنثَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَhtnُaʾْlٱ", "root": "انث"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 22, "text": "تِلْكَ إِذًا قِسْمَةٌ ضِيزَىٰٓ ", "translation": "That, then, is an unjust division.[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "تِلْكَ", "translit": "َkْlِt", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "إِذًا", "translit": "āًhdِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "قِسْمَةٌ", "translit": "ٌẗَmْsِq", "root": "قسم"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ضِيزَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَzyِḍ", "root": "ضيز"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 23, "text": "إِنْ هِىَ إِلَّآ أَسْمَآءٌ سَمَّيْتُمُوهَآ أَنتُمْ وَءَابَآؤُكُم مَّآ أَنزَلَ ٱللَّهُ بِهَا مِن سُلْطَٰنٍۚ إِن يَتَّبِعُونَ إِلَّا ٱلظَّنَّ وَمَا تَهْوَى ٱلْأَنفُسُۖ وَلَقَدْ جَآءَهُم مِّن رَّبِّهِمُ ٱلْهُدَىٰٓ ", "translation": "They are not but [mere] names you have named them - you and your forefathers - for which Allāh has sent down no authority. They follow not except assumption and what [their] souls desire, and there has already come to them from their Lord guidance.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنْ", "translit": "ْnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "هِىَ", "translit": "َàِh", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "إِلَّآ", "translit": "ٓāَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أَسْمَآءٌ", "translit": "ٌءٓāَmْsَaʾ", "root": "سمو"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "سَمَّيْتُمُوهَآ", "translit": "ٓāَhwُmُtْyَّmَs", "root": "سمو"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "أَنتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَءَابَآؤُكُم", "translit": "mُkُwʾٓāَbāَءَw", "root": "ابو"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "مَّآ", "translit": "ٓāَّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "أَنزَلَ", "translit": "َlَznَaʾ", "root": "نزل"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "بِهَا", "translit": "āَhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "سُلْطَٰنٍ", "translit": "ٍnَٰṭْlُs", "root": "سلط"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "إِن", "translit": "nِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "يَتَّبِعُونَ", "translit": "َnwُʿعِbَّtَy", "root": "تبع"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "ٱلظَّنَّ", "translit": "َّnَّẓlٱ", "root": "ظنن"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "تَهْوَى", "translit": "àَwْhَt", "root": "هوي"}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "ٱلْأَنفُسُ", "translit": "ُsُfnَaʾْlٱ", "root": "نفس"}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "وَلَقَدْ", "translit": "ْdَqَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "جَآءَهُم", "translit": "mُhَءٓāَjg", "root": "جيا"}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "رَّبِّهِمُ", "translit": "ُmِhِّbَّr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "ٱلْهُدَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَdُhْlٱ", "root": "هدي"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 24, "text": "أَمْ لِلْإِنسَٰنِ مَا تَمَنَّىٰ ", "translation": "Or is there for man whatever he wishes?", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَمْ", "translit": "ْmَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لِلْإِنسَٰنِ", "translit": "ِnَٰsnِiʾْlِl", "root": "انس"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "تَمَنَّىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَّnَmَt", "root": "مني"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 25, "text": "فَلِلَّهِ ٱلْءَاخِرَةُ وَٱلْأُولَىٰ ", "translation": "Rather, to Allāh belongs the Hereafter and the first [life].", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَلِلَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّlِlَf", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلْءَاخِرَةُ", "translit": "ُẗَrِhkāَءْlٱ", "root": "اخر"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَٱلْأُولَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlwُaʾْlٱَw", "root": "اول"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 26, "text": "وَكَم مِّن مَّلَكٍ فِى ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ لَا تُغْنِى شَفَٰعَتُهُمْ شَيْـًٔا إِلَّا مِنۢ بَعْدِ أَن يَأْذَنَ ٱللَّهُ لِمَن يَشَآءُ وَيَرْضَىٰٓ ", "translation": "And how many angels there are in the heavens whose intercession will not avail at all except [only] after Allāh has permitted [it] to whom He wills and approves.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَكَم", "translit": "mَkَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مَّلَكٍ", "translit": "ٍkَlَّm", "root": "ملك"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰwَٰmَّslٱ", "root": "سمو"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "تُغْنِى", "translit": "àِnْغُt", "root": "غني"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "شَفَٰعَتُهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhُtَʿعَٰfَhs", "root": "شفع"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "شَيْـًٔا", "translit": "āًٔـْyَhs", "root": "شيا"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "مِنۢ", "translit": "ۢnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "بَعْدِ", "translit": "ِdْʿعَb", "root": "بعد"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "أَن", "translit": "nَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "يَأْذَنَ", "translit": "َnَhdْaʾَy", "root": "اذن"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "ٱللَّهُ", "translit": "ُhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "لِمَن", "translit": "nَmِl", "root": ""}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "يَشَآءُ", "translit": "ُءٓāَhsَy", "root": "شيا"}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "وَيَرْضَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَḍْrَyَw", "root": "رضو"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 27, "text": "إِنَّ ٱلَّذِينَ لَا يُؤْمِنُونَ بِٱلْءَاخِرَةِ لَيُسَمُّونَ ٱلْمَلَٰٓئِكَةَ تَسْمِيَةَ ٱلْأُنثَىٰ ", "translation": "Indeed, those who do not believe in the Hereafter name the angels female names,", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "يُؤْمِنُونَ", "translit": "َnwُnِmْwʾُy", "root": "امن"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بِٱلْءَاخِرَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَrِhkāَءْlٱِb", "root": "اخر"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لَيُسَمُّونَ", "translit": "َnwُّmَsُyَl", "root": "سمو"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلْمَلَٰٓئِكَةَ", "translit": "َẗَkِyʾَٰٓlَmْlٱ", "root": "ملك"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "تَسْمِيَةَ", "translit": "َẗَyِmْsَt", "root": "سمو"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلْأُنثَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَhtnُaʾْlٱ", "root": "انث"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 28, "text": "وَمَا لَهُم بِهِۦ مِنْ عِلْمٍۖ إِن يَتَّبِعُونَ إِلَّا ٱلظَّنَّۖ وَإِنَّ ٱلظَّنَّ لَا يُغْنِى مِنَ ٱلْحَقِّ شَيْـًٔا ", "translation": "And they have thereof no knowledge. They follow not except assumption, and indeed, assumption avails not against the truth at all.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لَهُم", "translit": "mُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مِنْ", "translit": "ْnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "عِلْمٍ", "translit": "ٍmْlِʿع", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "إِن", "translit": "nِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "يَتَّبِعُونَ", "translit": "َnwُʿعِbَّtَy", "root": "تبع"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلظَّنَّ", "translit": "َّnَّẓlٱ", "root": "ظنن"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَإِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱلظَّنَّ", "translit": "َّnَّẓlٱ", "root": "ظنن"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "يُغْنِى", "translit": "àِnْغُy", "root": "غني"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "مِنَ", "translit": "َnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "ٱلْحَقِّ", "translit": "ِّqَḥْlٱ", "root": "حقق"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "شَيْـًٔا", "translit": "āًٔـْyَhs", "root": "شيا"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 29, "text": "فَأَعْرِضْ عَن مَّن تَوَلَّىٰ عَن ذِكْرِنَا وَلَمْ يُرِدْ إِلَّا ٱلْحَيَوٰةَ ٱلدُّنْيَا ", "translation": "So turn away from whoever turns his back on Our message and desires not except the worldly life.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَأَعْرِضْ", "translit": "ْḍِrْʿعَaʾَf", "root": "عرض"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "عَن", "translit": "nَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مَّن", "translit": "nَّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "تَوَلَّىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَّlَwَt", "root": "ولي"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "عَن", "translit": "nَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ذِكْرِنَا", "translit": "āَnِrْkِhd", "root": "ذكر"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَلَمْ", "translit": "ْmَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "يُرِدْ", "translit": "ْdِrُy", "root": "رود"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱلْحَيَوٰةَ", "translit": "َẗٰwَyَḥْlٱ", "root": "حيي"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱلدُّنْيَا", "translit": "āَyْnُّdlٱ", "root": "دنو"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 30, "text": "ذَٰلِكَ مَبْلَغُهُم مِّنَ ٱلْعِلْمِۚ إِنَّ رَبَّكَ هُوَ أَعْلَمُ بِمَن ضَلَّ عَن سَبِيلِهِۦ وَهُوَ أَعْلَمُ بِمَنِ ٱهْتَدَىٰ ", "translation": "That is their sum of knowledge. Indeed, your Lord is most knowing of who strays from His way, and He is most knowing of who is guided.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhd", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مَبْلَغُهُم", "translit": "mُhُغَlْbَm", "root": "بلغ"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مِّنَ", "translit": "َnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْعِلْمِ", "translit": "ِmْlِʿعْlٱ", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "رَبَّكَ", "translit": "َkَّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "هُوَ", "translit": "َwُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "أَعْلَمُ", "translit": "ُmَlْʿعَaʾ", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "بِمَن", "translit": "nَmِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ضَلَّ", "translit": "َّlَḍ", "root": "ضلل"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "عَن", "translit": "nَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "سَبِيلِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِlyِbَs", "root": "سبل"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "وَهُوَ", "translit": "َwُhَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "أَعْلَمُ", "translit": "ُmَlْʿعَaʾ", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "بِمَنِ", "translit": "ِnَmِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "ٱهْتَدَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَdَtْhٱ", "root": "هدي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 31, "text": "وَلِلَّهِ مَا فِى ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ وَمَا فِى ٱلْأَرْضِ لِيَجْزِىَ ٱلَّذِينَ أَسَٰٓـُٔوا۟ بِمَا عَمِلُوا۟ وَيَجْزِىَ ٱلَّذِينَ أَحْسَنُوا۟ بِٱلْحُسْنَى ", "translation": "And to Allāh belongs whatever is in the heavens and whatever is in the earth - that He may recompense those who do evil with [the penalty of] what they have done and recompense those who do good with the best [reward] -", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلِلَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّlِlَw", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰwَٰmَّslٱ", "root": "سمو"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْضِ", "translit": "ِḍْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "لِيَجْزِىَ", "translit": "َàِzْjgَyِl", "root": "جزي"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "أَسَٰٓـُٔوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُٔـَٰٓsَaʾ", "root": "سوا"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "بِمَا", "translit": "āَmِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "عَمِلُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlِmَʿع", "root": "عمل"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "وَيَجْزِىَ", "translit": "َàِzْjgَyَw", "root": "جزي"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "أَحْسَنُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnَsْḥَaʾ", "root": "حسن"}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "بِٱلْحُسْنَى", "translit": "àَnْsُḥْlٱِb", "root": "حسن"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 32, "text": "ٱلَّذِينَ يَجْتَنِبُونَ كَبَٰٓئِرَ ٱلْإِثْمِ وَٱلْفَوَٰحِشَ إِلَّا ٱللَّمَمَۚ إِنَّ رَبَّكَ وَٰسِعُ ٱلْمَغْفِرَةِۚ هُوَ أَعْلَمُ بِكُمْ إِذْ أَنشَأَكُم مِّنَ ٱلْأَرْضِ وَإِذْ أَنتُمْ أَجِنَّةٌ فِى بُطُونِ أُمَّهَٰتِكُمْۖ فَلَا تُزَكُّوٓا۟ أَنفُسَكُمْۖ هُوَ أَعْلَمُ بِمَنِ ٱتَّقَىٰٓ ", "translation": "Those who avoid the major sins and immoralities, only [committing] slight ones. Indeed, your Lord is vast in forgiveness. He was most knowing of you when He produced you from the earth and when you were fetuses in the wombs of your mothers. So do not claim yourselves to be pure; He is most knowing of who fears Him.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِينَ", "translit": "َnyِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَجْتَنِبُونَ", "translit": "َnwُbِnَtْjgَy", "root": "جنب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "كَبَٰٓئِرَ", "translit": "َrِyʾَٰٓbَk", "root": "كبر"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْإِثْمِ", "translit": "ِmْhtِiʾْlٱ", "root": "اثم"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَٱلْفَوَٰحِشَ", "translit": "َhsِḥَٰwَfْlٱَw", "root": "فحش"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱللَّمَمَ", "translit": "َmَmَّllٱ", "root": "لمم"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "رَبَّكَ", "translit": "َkَّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَٰسِعُ", "translit": "ُʿعِsَٰw", "root": "وسع"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱلْمَغْفِرَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَrِfْغَmْlٱ", "root": "غفر"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "هُوَ", "translit": "َwُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "أَعْلَمُ", "translit": "ُmَlْʿعَaʾ", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "بِكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "إِذْ", "translit": "ْhdِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 16, "arabic": "أَنشَأَكُم", "translit": "mُkَaʾَhsnَaʾ", "root": "نشا"}, {"id": 17, "arabic": "مِّنَ", "translit": "َnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 18, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْضِ", "translit": "ِḍْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 19, "arabic": "وَإِذْ", "translit": "ْhdِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 20, "arabic": "أَنتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 21, "arabic": "أَجِنَّةٌ", "translit": "ٌẗَّnِjgَaʾ", "root": "جنن"}, {"id": 22, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 23, "arabic": "بُطُونِ", "translit": "ِnwُṭُb", "root": "بطن"}, {"id": 24, "arabic": "أُمَّهَٰتِكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkِtَٰhَّmُaʾ", "root": "امم"}, {"id": 25, "arabic": "فَلَا", "translit": "āَlَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 26, "arabic": "تُزَكُّوٓا۟", "translit": "۟āٓwُّkَzُt", "root": "زكو"}, {"id": 27, "arabic": "أَنفُسَكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَsُfnَaʾ", "root": "نفس"}, {"id": 28, "arabic": "هُوَ", "translit": "َwُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 29, "arabic": "أَعْلَمُ", "translit": "ُmَlْʿعَaʾ", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 30, "arabic": "بِمَنِ", "translit": "ِnَmِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 31, "arabic": "ٱتَّقَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَqَّtٱ", "root": "وقي"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 33, "text": "أَفَرَءَيْتَ ٱلَّذِى تَوَلَّىٰ ", "translation": "Have you seen the one who turned away", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَفَرَءَيْتَ", "translit": "َtْyَءَrَfَaʾ", "root": "راي"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِى", "translit": "àِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "تَوَلَّىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَّlَwَt", "root": "ولي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 34, "text": "وَأَعْطَىٰ قَلِيلًا وَأَكْدَىٰٓ ", "translation": "And gave a little and [then] refrained?", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَأَعْطَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَṭْʿعَaʾَw", "root": "عطو"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "قَلِيلًا", "translit": "āًlyِlَq", "root": "قلل"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَأَكْدَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَdْkَaʾَw", "root": "كدي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 35, "text": "أَعِندَهُۥ عِلْمُ ٱلْغَيْبِ فَهُوَ يَرَىٰٓ ", "translation": "Does he have knowledge of the unseen, so he sees?[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَعِندَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَdnِʿعَaʾ", "root": "عند"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "عِلْمُ", "translit": "ُmْlِʿع", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْغَيْبِ", "translit": "ِbْyَغْlٱ", "root": "غيب"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "فَهُوَ", "translit": "َwُhَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "يَرَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَrَy", "root": "راي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 36, "text": "أَمْ لَمْ يُنَبَّأْ بِمَا فِى صُحُفِ مُوسَىٰ ", "translation": "Or has he not been informed of what was in the scriptures of Moses", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَمْ", "translit": "ْmَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لَمْ", "translit": "ْmَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يُنَبَّأْ", "translit": "ْaʾَّbَnُy", "root": "نبا"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "بِمَا", "translit": "āَmِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "صُحُفِ", "translit": "ِfُḥُṣ", "root": "صحف"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مُوسَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَswُm", "root": ""}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 37, "text": "وَإِبْرَٰهِيمَ ٱلَّذِى وَفَّىٰٓ ", "translation": "And [of] Abraham, who fulfilled [his obligations] -", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَإِبْرَٰهِيمَ", "translit": "َmyِhَٰrْbِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِى", "translit": "àِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَفَّىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَّfَw", "root": "وفي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 38, "text": "أَلَّا تَزِرُ وَازِرَةٌ وِزْرَ أُخْرَىٰ ", "translation": "That no bearer of burdens will bear the burden of another", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَلَّا", "translit": "āَّlَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "تَزِرُ", "translit": "ُrِzَt", "root": "وزر"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَازِرَةٌ", "translit": "ٌẗَrِzāَw", "root": "وزر"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وِزْرَ", "translit": "َrْzِw", "root": "وزر"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "أُخْرَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَrْhkُaʾ", "root": "اخر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 39, "text": "وَأَن لَّيْسَ لِلْإِنسَٰنِ إِلَّا مَا سَعَىٰ ", "translation": "And that there is not for man except that [good] for which he strives", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَأَن", "translit": "nَaʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لَّيْسَ", "translit": "َsْyَّl", "root": "ليس"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لِلْإِنسَٰنِ", "translit": "ِnَٰsnِiʾْlِl", "root": "انس"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "سَعَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَʿعَs", "root": "سعي"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 40, "text": "وَأَنَّ سَعْيَهُۥ سَوْفَ يُرَىٰ ", "translation": "And that his effort is going to be seen -", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَأَنَّ", "translit": "َّnَaʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "سَعْيَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَyْʿعَs", "root": "سعي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "سَوْفَ", "translit": "َfْwَs", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "يُرَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَrُy", "root": "راي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 41, "text": "ثُمَّ يُجْزَىٰهُ ٱلْجَزَآءَ ٱلْأَوْفَىٰ ", "translation": "Then he will be recompensed for it with the fullest recompense -", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ثُمَّ", "translit": "َّmُht", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يُجْزَىٰهُ", "translit": "ُhٰàَzْjgُy", "root": "جزي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْجَزَآءَ", "translit": "َءٓāَzَjgْlٱ", "root": "جزي"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْأَوْفَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَfْwَaʾْlٱ", "root": "وفي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 42, "text": "وَأَنَّ إِلَىٰ رَبِّكَ ٱلْمُنتَهَىٰ ", "translation": "And that to your Lord is the finality", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَأَنَّ", "translit": "َّnَaʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "إِلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "رَبِّكَ", "translit": "َkِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْمُنتَهَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَhَtnُmْlٱ", "root": "نهي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 43, "text": "وَأَنَّهُۥ هُوَ أَضْحَكَ وَأَبْكَىٰ ", "translation": "And that it is He who makes [one] laugh and weep", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَأَنَّهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَّnَaʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "هُوَ", "translit": "َwُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَضْحَكَ", "translit": "َkَḥْḍَaʾ", "root": "ضحك"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَأَبْكَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَkْbَaʾَw", "root": "بكي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 44, "text": "وَأَنَّهُۥ هُوَ أَمَاتَ وَأَحْيَا ", "translation": "And that it is He who causes death and gives life", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَأَنَّهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَّnَaʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "هُوَ", "translit": "َwُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَمَاتَ", "translit": "َtāَmَaʾ", "root": "موت"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَأَحْيَا", "translit": "āَyْḥَaʾَw", "root": "حيي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 45, "text": "وَأَنَّهُۥ خَلَقَ ٱلزَّوْجَيْنِ ٱلذَّكَرَ وَٱلْأُنثَىٰ ", "translation": "And that He creates the two mates - the male and female -", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَأَنَّهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَّnَaʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "خَلَقَ", "translit": "َqَlَhk", "root": "خلق"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلزَّوْجَيْنِ", "translit": "ِnْyَjgْwَّzlٱ", "root": "زوج"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلذَّكَرَ", "translit": "َrَkَّhdlٱ", "root": "ذكر"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَٱلْأُنثَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَhtnُaʾْlٱَw", "root": "انث"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 46, "text": "مِن نُّطْفَةٍ إِذَا تُمْنَىٰ ", "translation": "From a sperm-drop when it is emitted", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "نُّطْفَةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَfْṭُّn", "root": "نطف"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "إِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "تُمْنَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَnْmُt", "root": "مني"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 47, "text": "وَأَنَّ عَلَيْهِ ٱلنَّشْأَةَ ٱلْأُخْرَىٰ ", "translation": "And that [incumbent] upon Him is the other [i.e., next] creation.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَأَنَّ", "translit": "َّnَaʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "عَلَيْهِ", "translit": "ِhْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلنَّشْأَةَ", "translit": "َẗَaʾْhsَّnlٱ", "root": "نشا"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْأُخْرَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَrْhkُaʾْlٱ", "root": "اخر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 48, "text": "وَأَنَّهُۥ هُوَ أَغْنَىٰ وَأَقْنَىٰ ", "translation": "And that it is He who enriches and suffices", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَأَنَّهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَّnَaʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "هُوَ", "translit": "َwُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَغْنَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَnْغَaʾ", "root": "غني"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَأَقْنَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَnْqَaʾَw", "root": "قني"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 49, "text": "وَأَنَّهُۥ هُوَ رَبُّ ٱلشِّعْرَىٰ ", "translation": "And that it is He who is the Lord of Sirius[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَأَنَّهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَّnَaʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "هُوَ", "translit": "َwُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "رَبُّ", "translit": "ُّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلشِّعْرَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَrْʿعِّhslٱ", "root": "شعر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 50, "text": "وَأَنَّهُۥٓ أَهْلَكَ عَادًا ٱلْأُولَىٰ ", "translation": "And that He destroyed the first [people of] ʿAad", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَأَنَّهُۥٓ", "translit": "ٓۥُhَّnَaʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَهْلَكَ", "translit": "َkَlْhَaʾ", "root": "هلك"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "عَادًا", "translit": "āًdāَʿع", "root": "عود"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْأُولَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlwُaʾْlٱ", "root": "اول"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 51, "text": "وَثَمُودَا۟ فَمَآ أَبْقَىٰ ", "translation": "And Thamūd - and He did not spare [them] -", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَثَمُودَا۟", "translit": "۟āَdwُmَhtَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "فَمَآ", "translit": "ٓāَmَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَبْقَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَqْbَaʾ", "root": "بقي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 52, "text": "وَقَوْمَ نُوحٍ مِّن قَبْلُۖ إِنَّهُمْ كَانُوا۟ هُمْ أَظْلَمَ وَأَطْغَىٰ ", "translation": "And the people of Noah before. Indeed, it was they who were [even] more unjust and oppressing.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَقَوْمَ", "translit": "َmْwَqَw", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "نُوحٍ", "translit": "ٍḥwُn", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "قَبْلُ", "translit": "ُlْbَq", "root": "قبل"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "إِنَّهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "كَانُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "هُمْ", "translit": "ْmُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "أَظْلَمَ", "translit": "َmَlْẓَaʾ", "root": "ظلم"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَأَطْغَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَغْṭَaʾَw", "root": "طغي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 53, "text": "وَٱلْمُؤْتَفِكَةَ أَهْوَىٰ ", "translation": "And the overturned towns[1] He hurled down", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَٱلْمُؤْتَفِكَةَ", "translit": "َẗَkِfَtْwʾُmْlٱَw", "root": "افك"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَهْوَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَwْhَaʾ", "root": "هوي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 54, "text": "فَغَشَّىٰهَا مَا غَشَّىٰ ", "translation": "And covered them by that which He covered.[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَغَشَّىٰهَا", "translit": "āَhٰàَّhsَغَf", "root": "غشو"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "غَشَّىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَّhsَغ", "root": "غشو"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 55, "text": "فَبِأَىِّ ءَالَآءِ رَبِّكَ تَتَمَارَىٰ ", "translation": "Then which of the favors of your Lord do you doubt?", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَبِأَىِّ", "translit": "ِّàَaʾِbَf", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ءَالَآءِ", "translit": "ِءٓāَlāَء", "root": "الو"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "رَبِّكَ", "translit": "َkِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "تَتَمَارَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَrāَmَtَt", "root": "مري"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 56, "text": "هَٰذَا نَذِيرٌ مِّنَ ٱلنُّذُرِ ٱلْأُولَىٰٓ ", "translation": "This [Prophet (ﷺ)] is a warner from [i.e., like] the former warners.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "هَٰذَا", "translit": "āَhdَٰh", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "نَذِيرٌ", "translit": "ٌryِhdَn", "root": "نذر"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مِّنَ", "translit": "َnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلنُّذُرِ", "translit": "ِrُhdُّnlٱ", "root": "نذر"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلْأُولَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَlwُaʾْlٱ", "root": "اول"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 57, "text": "أَزِفَتِ ٱلْءَازِفَةُ ", "translation": "The Approaching Day has approached.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَزِفَتِ", "translit": "ِtَfِzَaʾ", "root": "ازف"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلْءَازِفَةُ", "translit": "ُẗَfِzāَءْlٱ", "root": "ازف"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 58, "text": "لَيْسَ لَهَا مِن دُونِ ٱللَّهِ كَاشِفَةٌ ", "translation": "Of it, [from those] besides Allāh, there is no remover.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "لَيْسَ", "translit": "َsْyَl", "root": "ليس"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لَهَا", "translit": "āَhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "دُونِ", "translit": "ِnwُd", "root": "دون"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "كَاشِفَةٌ", "translit": "ٌẗَfِhsāَk", "root": "كشف"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 59, "text": "أَفَمِنْ هَٰذَا ٱلْحَدِيثِ تَعْجَبُونَ ", "translation": "Then at this statement do you wonder?", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَفَمِنْ", "translit": "ْnِmَfَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "هَٰذَا", "translit": "āَhdَٰh", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْحَدِيثِ", "translit": "ِhtyِdَḥْlٱ", "root": "حدث"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "تَعْجَبُونَ", "translit": "َnwُbَjgْʿعَt", "root": "عجب"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 60, "text": "وَتَضْحَكُونَ وَلَا تَبْكُونَ ", "translation": "And you laugh and do not weep", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَتَضْحَكُونَ", "translit": "َnwُkَḥْḍَtَw", "root": "ضحك"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "وَلَا", "translit": "āَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "تَبْكُونَ", "translit": "َnwُkْbَt", "root": "بكي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 61, "text": "وَأَنتُمْ سَٰمِدُونَ ", "translation": "While you are proudly sporting?[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَأَنتُمْ", "translit": "ْmُtnَaʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "سَٰمِدُونَ", "translit": "َnwُdِmَٰs", "root": "سمد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 62, "text": "فَٱسْجُدُوا۟ لِلَّهِ وَٱعْبُدُوا۟۩ ", "translation": "So prostrate to Allāh and worship [Him].", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَٱسْجُدُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُdُjgْsٱَf", "root": "سجد"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لِلَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّlِl", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَٱعْبُدُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُdُbْʿعٱَw", "root": "عبد"}], "hasIhya": false}], "tafsir": {"3": [{"arabic": "الله عليه وسلم إلا بحقيقة الحق فإنه لا ينطق عن الهوى إن هو إلا وحي يوحى وهذا كقوله صلى الله عليه وسلم الرؤيا الصالحة جزء من ستة وأربعين جزءامن النبوة (2) فإنه تقدير تحقيق لا محالة لكن ليس في قوة غيره أن يعرف علة تلك النسبة إلا بتخمين فأما بالتحقيق فلا إذ يعلم أن النبوة عبارة عما يختص به النبي ويفارق به غيره وهو يختص بأنواع من الخواص أحدها أن يعرف حقائق الأمور المتعلقة بالله وصفاته والملائكة والدار الآخرة لا كما يعلمه غيره بل مخالفا له بكثرة المعلومات وبزيادة اليقين والتحقيق والكشف والثاني أن له في نفسه صفة بها تتم", "english": "As for the verses [of the Qur'an], that is indicated by His saying, the Exalted: {«لِلْفُقَرَاءِ الْمُهَاجِرِينَ الَّذِينَ أُخْرِجُوا مِنْ دِيَارِهِمْ وَأَمْوَالِهِمْ»} [To the poor emigrants who were driven out of their homes and their wealth] (59:8), to the end of the verse. And He said, the Exalted: {«لِلْفُقَرَاءِ الَّذِينَ أُحْصِرُوا فِي سَبِيلِ اللَّهِ لَا يَسْتَطِيعُونَ ضَرْبًا فِي الْأَرْضِ»} [To the poor who are confined in the way of Allah, unable to travel in the land] (2:273). He directed the discourse in the context of praise, then He advanced their description with poverty before their description with emigration and confinement. In this there is an evident indication of the praise of poverty.", "book_title": "Poverty and Abstinence", "section_title_ar": "Section 1069", "section_title_en": "The Virtue of Poverty: Scriptural Evidences and Their Interpretation", "section_index": 1069, "anchors": "- Root: فقر (F-Q-R)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of need, indigence, and the state of lacking possessions; also the notches of a vertebra or the joints of the back, indicating structural dependence.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Poverty is of four degrees: (1) essential neediness before God (universal to all creatures), (2) lack of worldly goods (the subject here), (3) voluntary renunciation (zuhd), and (4) the perfection of the heart's detachment. The praise of poverty here targets the second and third degrees, not destitution that breeds discontent.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) poverty is the absence of wealth; metaphorical (Majaz) poverty is the soul's humility and its \"notches\" of dependence on God. The Prophet's praise of the \"poor\" (fuqara') must be read as praise of the virtuous poor (mutafaQQir), not the covetous.\n- Root: هجر (H-J-R)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To abandon, emigrate, or sever ties; the act of leaving one's homeland for a religious purpose.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Hijrah (emigration) is a physical act of devotion, but the Quranic verse pairs it with poverty, elevating the state of neediness as a spiritual station above the mere act of relocation.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): The verse's structure (taqdim) places \"poverty\" before \"emigration\" and \"confinement,\" signaling its superior rank in the divine estimation.\n- Root: قدر (Q-D-R)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Measure, decree, power, and estimation; the act of determining a precise limit or quantity.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): When applied to God, Qadar is His eternal decree; when applied to the Prophet's speech, it denotes precise, divinely-inspired measurement—never arbitrary or haphazard.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: to measure out (qaddara). Metaphorical: the \"measure\" of a thing's worth or rank. The Prophet's numerical estimates (e.g., 500 years, 46 parts) are literal measures of spiritual realities, though their exact rationale may be hidden from non-prophetic intellects.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The Quranic construction {للفقراء المهاجرين} uses prepositional fronting (taqdim) to restrict the praise to a specific class, and the sequence of descriptors (poverty before hijrah) establishes a hierarchy of virtues through syntactic ordering.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Poverty and Abstinence'", "badge": "Insight"}], "5": [{"arabic": "وهو المراد بقوله تعالى {علمه شديد القوى ذو مرة فاستوى وهو بالأفق الأعلى} إلى آخر هذه الآيات وفي مثل هذه الأحوال من الصفاء يقع الإطلاع على ضمائر القلوب وقد يعبر عن ذلك الإطلاع بالتفرس ولذلك قال صلى الله عليه وسلم اتقوا فراسة المؤمن فإنه ينظر بنور الله (2) وقد حكى أن رجلا من المجوس كان يدور على المسلمين ويقول ما معنى قول النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم اتقوا فراسة المؤمن فكان يذكر له تفسيره فلا يقنعه ذلك حتى انتهى إلى بعض المشايخ من الصوفية فسأله فقال له معناه أن تقطع الزنار الذي على وسطك تحت ثوبك فقال صدقت هذا معناه وأسلم وقا", "english": "For this reason, he (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"Beware the discernment of the believer, for he looks with the light of God.\" (2)", "book_title": "Audition and Ecstasy", "section_title_ar": "Section 509", "section_title_en": "The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 509: On the Vision of Spiritual Realities Through Purity of Heart", "section_index": 509, "anchors": "- Root: صفا (ṣafā)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of clarity, purity, and transparency — free from turbidity or admixture.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Denotes the heart's purification from base attributes, rendering it receptive to divine disclosures (mukāshafāt).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: clarity of water or wine. Metaphorical: purity of the heart as the condition for spiritual perception.\n- Root: مثل (mathala)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To stand before, to appear, to take shape or form.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Used for representation and formation — the appearing of a thing in a form (ṣūrah) that may or may not correspond to its true reality.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: standing erect before someone. Metaphorical: the self-representation of spiritual beings (arwāḥ) in sensible forms perceptible to the purified heart.\n- Root: نظر (naẓar)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Vision, sight, and also reflection and consideration.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Distinguishes between baṣar (physical sight) and baṣīrah (insight); the latter perceives spiritual realities (malakūt).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: seeing with the eye. Metaphorical: \"looking with the light of God\" (al-naẓar bi-nūr Allāh) — perception through divine illumination.\n- Root: فرس (farasa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To prey upon, to seize — the ferāsah (discernment) is so named because it seizes upon the inner reality.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The intuitive perception of inner states — a form of inspired knowledge (ʿilm ladunī).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the lion preying on its quarry. Metaphorical: the believer's discernment that pierces through outward appearances to inward truths.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — Used to confine the activity of devils to hearts filled with blameworthy attributes, and to restrict the vision of the Kingdom of Heaven to purified hearts.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Audition and Ecstasy'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "11": [{"arabic": "قال الله تعالى ما كذب الفؤاد ما رأى وقال تعالى وكذلك نرى إبراهيم ملكوت السموات والأرض الآية وسمي ضده عمى فقال تعالى فإنها لا تعمى الأبصار ولكن تعمي القلوب التي في الصدور وقال تعالى ومن كان في هذه أعمى فهو في الآخرة أعمى وأضل سبيلا وهذه الأمور التي كشفت للأنبياء بعضها كان بالبصر وبعضها كان بالبصيرة وسمي الكل رؤية وبالجملة من لم تكن بصيرته الباطنة ثاقبة لم يعلق به من الدين إلا قشوره وأمثلته دون لبابه وحقائقه فهذه أقسام ما ينطلق اسم العقل عليها", "english": "I say: Know that the washing of the eye is forbidden — that is, the eye of insight (ʿayn al-baṣīrah) is not to be washed with the water of blind imitation and mere hearsay, for it is the organ by which the realities of the intellect are disclosed. And the divisions of what the name \"intellect\" (al-ʿaql) is applied to have become clear: it is said of the inborn faculty (al-gharīzah), and it is said of the knowledge that is acquired through that faculty, and it is said of the state of that knowledge when it governs the soul, and it is said of the very actions that proceed from that governance.", "book_title": "Book of Knowledge", "section_title_ar": "Section 68", "section_title_en": "The Washing of the Eye is Forbidden — On the True Meaning of Intellect (ʿAql) and Its Divisions", "section_index": 68, "anchors": "- Root: عقل (ʿAql)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes binding, restraining, and holding back — the intellect is that which restrains the soul from base desires and prevents it from straying.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العقل يقال للقوة المتهيئة لقبول العلم، ويقال للعلم الذي يستفيده الإنسان بتلك القوة عقل\" — al-ʿaql is said of the faculty prepared to receive knowledge, and it is also said of the knowledge acquired by that faculty. It is the instrument of divine discernment and the locus of taklīf (moral responsibility).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): the inborn faculty of discernment. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"نخلة لا تعقل الإبار إذا لم تقبله\" — a palm tree that does not accept pollination is said to be \"not to ʿaqil\" the pollen, extending the root to the sense of \"accepting/receiving.\" Thus, knowledge is called ʿaql metaphorically as its fruit.\n- Root: بصر (Baṣar)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Sight, perception — both of the eye and of the heart.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"البصر يقال للجارحة الناظرة... ويقال لقوة القلب المدركة: بصيرة وبصر\" — baṣar is said of the seeing organ, and of the perceiving power of the heart: baṣīrah and baṣar. The inner sight (baṣīrah) perceives immaterial realities.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: ocular vision. Metaphorical: \"هذه آية مبصرة\" — this is an illuminating sign; \"واجعلني بصيرة عليهم أي رقيبا وشاهدا\" — make me a watcher over them. The inner eye perceives what the outer eye cannot.\n- Root: فطر (Faṭara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To split, cleave, originate, create — the primordial act of divine creation.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"أصل الفطر: الشق طولا... وفطر الله الخلق\" — the root is splitting lengthwise; God's \"fatr\" of creation is His originating of things. \"فطرة الله التي فطر الناس عليها\" — the primordial disposition upon which God originated mankind.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"فطر الله الخلق، وهو فاطر السموات: مبتدعها\" — God originated creation; He is the Originator of the heavens. \"وكل مولود يولد على الفطرة\" — every newborn is born upon the fiṭrah, i.e., the innate disposition receptive to the religion of truth.\n- Root: نفس (Nafs)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Self, soul, spirit; the innermost reality of a thing.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النفس: الروح... وقوله: تعلم ما في نفسي ولا أعلم ما في نفسك\" — nafs is the spirit/soul; \"You know what is in my nafs and I know not what is in Your nafs\" — the nafs is the seat of inner assent and recognition.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the self. Metaphorical: \"دفق نفسه أي دمه\" — he poured out his nafs, i.e., his blood. The nafs is the locus of the primordial covenant (mīthāq).\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The construction \"إنما العاقل من آمن بالله\" restricts the predicate: the intellect is not merely a faculty of cleverness but is defined by its fruit — faith and God-consciousness (taqwā).", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Book of Knowledge'", "badge": "Insight"}], "23": [{"arabic": "أصلا وإنما يتبعون الظن وما تهوى الأنفس ولقد جاءهم من ربهم الهدى وهو سبب اليقين إلا أنهم معرضون عنه فإذن الجبن والجراءة غرائز ولا ينفع اليقين معها فهي أحد الأسباب التي تضاد حال التوكل كما أن ضعف اليقين بالخصال الأربعة أحد الأسباب وإذا اجتمعت هذه الأسباب حصلت الثقة بالله تعالى وقد قيل مكتوب في التوراة ملعون من ثقته إنسان مثله وقد قال صلى الله عليه وسلم", "english": "So when you have understood tawakkul in this example, then measure upon it the tawakkul upon Allah — exalted be He. For if there is established in your soul — through unveiling (kashf) or through decisive belief (iʿtiqād jāzim) — that there is no agent (fāʿil) except Allah, as has preceded; and you believe, along with that, in the completeness of [His] knowledge and power (al-ʿilm wa al-qudrah) sufficient for the servants; then the completeness of [His] care, affection, and mercy (al-ʿināyah wa al-ʿaṭf wa al-raḥmah) toward all the servants and each individual; and that there is no power beyond the limit of His power, no knowledge beyond the limit of His knowledge, and no care and mercy beyond the limit of His care for you and His mercy to you — then your heart will inevitably rely upon Him alone, and will not turn to any other in any respect, nor to itself, nor to its own might and strength. For there is no might and no power except through Allah (lā ḥawla wa lā quwwata illā billāh), as has preceded in [the book of] Tawḥīd at the mention of movement and power — for \"might\" (al-ḥawl) is an expression for movement, and \"power\" (al-quwwah) is an expression for capability (al-qudrah).", "book_title": "Monotheism and Reliance", "section_title_ar": "Section 1117", "section_title_en": "The Reality of Tawakkul — The Interplay of Certainty (Yaqīn), Heart's Stillness (Ṭuma'nīnah), and the Four Attributes of the Trustee (Al-Wakīl)", "section_index": 1117, "anchors": "- Root: وكل (W-K-L)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of delegating, entrusting, and appointing a deputy (wakīl) to act on one's behalf; the wakīl is the one relied upon for a task.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Tawakkul is to rely upon another and make him a deputy over your affair; true tawakkul on Allah is to deem Him sufficient (ḥasbunā Allāh) as the sole Guardian of your state.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) usage is delegation of a concrete task; metaphorical (majāz) usage is the heart's utter dependence on the trustee, even to the point of describing a weak man as \"tikl\" (one who leans entirely on others).\n- Root: قلب (Q-L-B)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The heart as the organ of constant turning and overturning (taqlīb); its states fluctuate and shift.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Qalb is the turning of a thing from one state to another; the heart's \"turning\" (inqilāb) is its movement between conditions of fear, hope, certainty, and agitation.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: to flip a thing over; metaphorical: the heart's disposition is \"turned\" by overwhelming fancies (awhām) and fears, just as a merchant \"turns over\" goods to inspect their true condition.\n- Root: نفس (N-F-S)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The self or soul; that which is the seat of inclination and emotional response.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The soul (nafs) is the subtle spiritual reality (laṭīfah) that follows imagination (khayāl) and finds rest only through it; the \"tranquil soul\" (al-nafs al-muṭma'innah) is its final perfected state.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the self as animate essence; metaphorical: the soul's \"flowing out\" (nafāsah) as preciousness, and its agitation as a type of \"breathlessness\" from fear.\n- Root: يقن (Y-Q-N)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Certainty as the absence of doubt; firm conviction.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Yaqīn is a quality of knowledge above mere cognition (ma'rifah); it is the stillness of understanding with the fixity of judgment — as in \"knowledge of certainty\" (ʿilm al-yaqīn), \"eye of certainty\" (ʿayn al-yaqīn), and \"truth of certainty\" (ḥaqq al-yaqīn).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: to be certain of a matter (yaqina al-amr); it is the firmest form of knowing, distinct from mere opinion (ẓann).\n- Sibawayh Rule: Restriction and Focused Predication (al-ḥaṣr wa al-taqyīd bi-innamā) — The sentence \"فالسكون في القلب شيء واليقين شيء آخر\" (Stillness in the heart is one thing, and certainty is another thing) uses two nominal sentences with contrastive focus to establish a strict ontological distinction between two distinct realities (ṭuma'nīnah vs. yaqīn), each with its own predicate, preventing conflation.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Monotheism and Reliance'", "badge": "Insight"}], "29": [{"arabic": "قوله عز وجل وهم في غفلة معرضون وفي قوله فأعرض عمن تولى عن ذكرنا ولم يرد إلا الحياة الدنيا وفي قوله تعالى ومن لم يتب فأولئك هم الظالمون إلى غير ذلك من الآيات وكان داخلا في معنى قوله عز وجل ومنهم أميون لا يعلمون الكتاب إلا أماني يعني التلاوة المجردة وقوله عز وجل وكأين من آية في السموات والأرض يمرون عليها وهم عنها معرضون لأن القرآن هو المبين لتلك الآيات في السموات والأرض ومهما تجاوزها ولم يتأثر بها كان معرضا عنها ولذلك قيل إن من لم يكن متصفا بأخلاق القرآن فإذا قرأ القرآن ناداه الله تعالى مالك ولكلامي وأنت معرض عني دع عنك كلامي إن لم ت", "english": "And at the mention of God, His attributes, and His names, he bows low in humility before His majesty and in apprehension of His greatness. And at the mention of the disbelievers' sayings — those things impossible for God, Mighty and Majestic, such as their attributing to Him a son or a consort — he lowers his voice and inwardly recoils in shame at the ugliness of their speech. And at the description of Paradise, his innermost being surges forth with longing for it. And at the description of the Fire, his limbs tremble in fear of it. For when the Messenger of God, peace be upon him, said to Ibn Mas'ud, \"Recite to me,\" he said: \"I began reciting Surah al-Nisa'. When I reached {فكيف إذا جئنا من كل أمة بشهيد وجئنا بك على هؤلاء شهيدا} [How then shall it be when We bring from every nation a witness, and We bring you as a witness against these?] (4:41), I saw his two eyes overflowing with tears. He then said to me: 'That is enough for you now.'\" And this was because the witnessing of that very state had wholly overwhelmed his heart.", "book_title": "Patience and Gratitude", "section_title_ar": "Section 230", "section_title_en": "The States of the Heart During Qur'anic Recitation — The Condition of the True Recieter", "section_index": 230, "anchors": "- Root: وجل (w-j-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root meaning of trembling fear, trepidation of the heart\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — the inward apprehension of fear; cited in {إنما المؤمنون الذين إذا ذكر الله وجلت قلوبهم}\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"رجل وجل، وقوم وجال\" — a man fearful, a people fearful; the state of the heart's trembling before divine majesty\n- Root: وصف (w-ṣ-f)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Describing a thing by its attributes and qualities\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوصف: ذكر الشيء بحليته ونعته\" — description is the mention of a thing by its adornment and attribute; may be true or false\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal vs Metaphorical: \"وجهها يصف الحسن\" — metaphorical usage where the face itself \"describes\" beauty; likewise, the tongue may \"describe\" falsehood, as in {ولا تقولوا لما تصف ألسنتكم الكذب}\n- Root: وفي (w-f-y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Completeness, fulfillment, reaching full measure\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوافي: الذي بلغ التمام\" — that which attains completion; \"أوفى: إذا تمم العهد ولم ينقض حفظه\" — fulfilled the covenant without breach\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"درهم واف\" — a full-weight dirham; Metaphorical: \"أوفى على المائة إذا زاد عليها\" — surpassed the hundred\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The separation between the governing preposition and its object by intervening clauses, as in \"فلما بلغت {فكيف إذا جئنا...}\" where the temporal clause governs the narrative sequence", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Patience and Gratitude'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "وقال عز وجل فأعرض عمن تولى عن ذكرنا ولم يرد إلا الحياة الدنيا ذلك مبلغهم من العلم فالجمع بين كمال الاستبصار في مصالح الدنيا والدين لا يكاد يتيسر إلا لمن رسخه الله لتدبير عباده في معاشهم ومعادهم وهم الأنبياء المؤيدون بروح القدس المستمدون من القوة الإلهية التي تتسع لجميع الأمور ولا تضيق عنها فأما قلوب سائر الخلق فإنها إذا استقلت بأمر الدنيا انصرفت عن الآخرة وقصرت عن الاستكمال فيها", "english": "They left [the vessels] upon the road — why do you not return them to their places? So they said to him: \"Those vessels are in their places; rather, it is you who cannot find the way, due to your blindness. The wonder of you is that you do not attribute your stumbling to your blindness, but rather attribute it to the shortcoming of another.\" Such is the relation of the religious sciences to the rational sciences.", "book_title": "Poverty and Abstinence", "section_title_ar": "Section 588", "section_title_en": "The Heart's Relation to the Divisions of the Rational, Religious, Worldly, and Otherworldly Sciences", "section_index": 588, "anchors": "- Root: عقل (ʿaql)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The faculty prepared for the reception of knowledge; also the knowledge acquired by that faculty.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العقل يقال للقوة المتهيئة لقبول العلم، ويقال للعلم الذي يستفيده الإنسان بتلك القوة عقل\" — the intellect is the power prepared to receive knowledge, and the knowledge one acquires by that power is also called ʿaql.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"ما لفلان مقول، ولا معقول\" — one who has neither speech nor understanding. Metaphorical: \"نخلة لا تعقل الإبار\" — a palm that does not accept pollination, i.e., does not take it in.\n- Root: قلب (qalb)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The heart; that which is ever-turning and fluctuating.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the turning of a thing from one state to another; the heart is so named for its constant turning of resolutions and states.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"قلب الشيء قلبا: حوله عن وجهه\" — to turn a thing from its face. Metaphorical: \"رجل قلب حول: يقلب الأمور ويحتال\" — a man who turns matters over and devises stratagems.\n- Root: دنا (danā)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Nearness in place, time, or rank.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الدنو: القرب بالذات، أو بالحكم\" — nearness by essence or by judgment; the worldly life (al-dunyā) is so named for its nearness and precedence.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"هو دنيء من الأدنياء\" — base and contemptible; the dunyā is that which is lower and nearer.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — used to restrict the predicate exclusively to the subject, as in the Prophetic statement restricting the majority of Paradise's inhabitants to the \"bullah\" (those seemingly foolish in worldly matters).", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Poverty and Abstinence'", "badge": "Insight"}], "31": [{"arabic": "يقول ليجزى الذي أساءوا بما عملوا ويجزى الذي أحسنوا بالحسنى وقال تعالى {ووضع الكتاب فترى المجرمين مشفقين مما فيه} الآية جعلنا الله وإياكم عاملين بكتابة متبعين لأوليائه حتى يحلنا وإياكم دار المقامة من فضله إنه حميد مجيد وقال بعض الحكماء الأيام سهام والناس أغراض والدهر يرميك كل يوم بسهامه ويخترمك بلياليه وأيامه حتى يستغرق جميع أجزائك فكيف بقاء سلامتك مع وقوع الأيام بك وسرعة الليالي في بدنك لو كشف لك عما أحدثت الأيام فيك من النقص لاستوحشت من كل يوم يأتي عليك واستثقلت ممر الساعة بك ولكن تدبير الله فوق تدبير ا", "english": "And indeed there has come the narration from him, from his Lord, Mighty and Glorious, that He said to Moses, peace be upon him: \"When you see the rich man approaching, say: 'A sin whose punishment has been hastened.' And when you see the poor man approaching, say: 'Welcome to the badge of the righteous.'\" And if you wish, you may follow the possessor of the Spirit and the Word, Jesus son of Mary, peace be upon him — for he used to say: \"My condiment is hunger, my badge is fear, my garment is wool, my warmth in winter is the rising places of the sun, my lamp is the moon, my mount is my two feet, and my food and my fruit is what the earth brings forth. I pass the night and I possess nothing, and I rise in the morning and I possess nothing — and there is no one upon the earth richer than I.\"", "book_title": "Condemnation of the World", "section_title_ar": "Section 737", "section_title_en": "The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 737: On the Censure of This World and the Description of Its Perils", "section_index": 737, "anchors": "- Root: دنو (danuww)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"دنا فهو دانٍ\" — proximity, nearness; the world (al-dunyā) is so named because it is nearer and lower, deferred from the Hereafter.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الدنو: القرب بالذات، أو بالحكم\" — proximity by essence or by decree; used of place, time, and station. The \"nearer\" (al-adnā) is contrasted with the greater and the better.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical nearness. Metaphorical: the lower, contemptible station — \"أهل الدناءة هم أهل الشناءة\" (the people of baseness are the people of abhorrence).\n- Root: يوم (yawm)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"اليوم\" — a day; also any period of time.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"اليوم يعبر به عن وقت طلوع الشمس إلى غروبها. وقد يعبر به عن مدة من الزمان أي مدة كانت\" — the day is the time from sunrise to sunset, or any duration whatsoever. \"وذكرهم بأيام الله\" — the days of God are His mighty deeds and calamities upon the disbelievers.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the solar day. Metaphorical: \"أيام العرب\" — their battles and vicissitudes; \"أيام الله\" — His punitive visitations.\n- Root: وقد (waqada)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"وقدت النار تقد وقودا ووقدا\" — the fire blazed; al-wuqūd is the fuel.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"وقودها الناس والحجارة\" — the fuel of the Fire is men and stones; the one who kindles it for himself (istawqada) is he who seeks its light.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: kindling a fire. Metaphorical: \"قلب وقاد\" — a blazing, keen heart; \"وقد الحصى\" — the pebbles became scorching hot (i.e., the intensity of the sun's heat).\n- Root: ألك (alaka)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"المألكة: الرسالة، لأنها تؤلك في الفم\" — the message (risālah) is called ma'lakah because it is chewed/worked in the mouth.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الملائكة تقع على الواحد والجمع\" — angels (malā'ikah) are so named as bearers of messages; \"الله يصطفي من الملائكة رسلا\" {«الله يصطفي من الملائكة رسلا»} — God chooses messengers from the angels.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: chewing (as a horse chews its bit). Metaphorical: delivering a message — the angel is the one who delivers the divine communication.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — \"إنما\" restricts the predicate to the subject alone, negating it of all else. Applied in the sermon: \"إنما يتزين لي أوليائي بالذل والخوف\" — My friends adorn themselves for Me ONLY with humility, fear, and submission — restricting their adornment to these alone, excluding all worldly ornament.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Condemnation of the World'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "قوله لله ما في السموات وما في الأرض إلى آخرها وقل هو الله أحد خمس عشرة مرة (3) وصف من ثوابه في الحديث ما يخرج عن الحصر وقال كرز بن وبرة وهو من الأبدال قلت للخضر عليه السلام علمني شيئا أعمله في كل ليلة فقال إذا صليت المغرب فقم إلى وقت صلاة العشاء مصليا من غير أن تكلم أحدا وأقبل على صلاتك التي أنت فيها وسلم من كل ركعتين واقرأ في كل ركعة فاتحة الكتاب مرة وقل هو الله أحد ثلاثا فإن فرغت من صلاتك انصرف إلى منزلك ولا تكلم أحدا وصل ركعتين واقرأ فاتحة الكتاب وقل هو الله أحد سبع مرات في كل ر", "english": "The Messenger of God (peace be upon him) said — according to what ʿĀ'ishah (may God be pleased with her) narrated: \"Verily, the most excellent of prayers in the sight of God is the Maghrib prayer. He has not exempted from it either the traveler or the resident. By it He opened the prayer of the night, and by it He sealed the prayer of the day. Whoever prays the Maghrib and prays after it two units (rakʿatayn), God will build for him a palace (qaṣr) in Paradise.\"", "book_title": "Arrangement of Litanies", "section_title_ar": "Section 271", "section_title_en": "The Book of the Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 271: On the Virtue of the Maghrib Prayer and the Facilitating Causes for the Night Vigil (Qiyām al-Layl)", "section_index": 271, "anchors": "- Root: غرب (gh-r-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of disappearance, setting, and the west; al-ghurāb is the setting of the sun.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الغرب: غيبوبة الشمس\" — the occultation of the sun; the Maghrib prayer is named for its time, marking the transition from the day's light into the night's dominion.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage denotes the sun's setting; metaphorically (Majaz), it signifies a boundary or point of transition — here, the temporal boundary between the daytime prayers and the night vigil.\n- Root: رسل (r-s-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Sending forth with gentleness and ease; a messenger (rasūl) is one dispatched.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"أصل الرسل: الانبعاث على التؤدة\" — the root denotes a measured, deliberate sending forth; from this derives the concept of the prophetic messenger.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"أرسل الله في الأمم رسلا\" (God sent messengers to the nations). Metaphorical: \"أرسل الله عليهم العذاب\" (God loosed chastisement upon them) — here, the ḥadīth's \"أرسل\" (in the chain) is literal transmission.\n- Root: ركع (r-k-')\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Bowing; the physical posture of inclination in prayer.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الركوع: الانحناء\" — used literally for the prayer posture and metaphorically for humility and submission before God.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"شيخ راكع: منحن من الكبر\" (an old man bowed with age). Metaphorical: \"لغبت الإبل حتى ركعت\" (camels fatigued until they knelt) — in the ḥadīth, \"ركعات\" (units of prayer) are named for the bowing that defines them.\n- Root: صلا (ṣ-l-w)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root of prayer (ṣalāh); also linked to the fire of kindling (taṣliyah).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"أصل الصلي الإيقاد بالنار\" — the root denotes kindling fire; the ṣalāh is so named for the \"burning\" of devotion or for the supplication that \"kindles\" the heart toward God.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): The term ṣalāh in the Sacred Law is a transferred (naql) technical designation for the specific acts of bowing, prostration, and recitation — distinct from its lexical sense of supplication (duʿā').\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The rule governing the permissibility of interposing elements between a preposition and its object, as in \"لم يحطها عن مسافر ولا عن مقيم\" where the pronominal object (ها) is separated from the second prepositional phrase (عن مقيم) by the conjunction, maintaining grammatical cohesion through parallel structure.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Arrangement of Litanies'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "32": [{"arabic": "ومنها تزكية النفس قال الله سبحانه وتعالى فلا تزكوا أنفسكم هو أعلم بمن اتقى وقيل لحكيم ما الصدق القبيح فقال ثناء المرء على نفسه ولا يخلو المناظر من الثناء على نفسه بالقوة والغلبة والتقدم على الأقران ولا ينفك في أثناء المناظرة عن قوله لست ممن يخفى عليه أمثال هذه الأمور وأنا المتفنن في العلوم والمستقل بالأصول وحفظ الأحاديث وغير ذلك مما يتمدح به تارة على سبيل الصلف وتارة للحاجة إلى ترويج كلامه ومعلوم أن الصلف والتمدح مذمومان شرعا وعقلا ومنها التجسس وتتبع عورات الناس وقد قال تعالى ولا تجسسوا والمناظر لا ينفك عن طلب عثرات أقرا", "english": "**QUAD_ANCHORS:**\n- Root: نظر (naẓara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to look, to examine, to consider; al-munāẓarah is mutual examination/argumentation between two parties.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: naẓar as intellectual contemplation (taʾammul bi-l-baṣīrah); munāẓarah as dialectical exchange.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: sitting face-to-face in debate; Metaphorical: disputation for victory.\n- Root: غلب (ghalaba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to overcome, to conquer, to prevail.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-ghalabah as seeking to dominate another in argument.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: victory in combat; Metaphorical: intellectual supremacy.\n- Root: حسد (ḥasada)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to envy, to wish removal of blessings from another.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: envy as wishing deprivation of blessings from the deserving — among the basest traits.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: envy of another's blessing; Metaphorical: consuming fire of the soul.\n- Root: كبر (kabura)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to be great, to be haughty.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-kibr as self-elevation above others; condemned by God.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: greatness; Metaphorical: arrogance and pride.\n- Root: غيب (ghāba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to be absent, to be hidden.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-ghībah as mentioning another with what he dislikes in his absence — likened to eating carrion.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: backbiting; Metaphorical: consuming the flesh of one's brother.\n- Root: راءى (rāʾā)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to show off, to display.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-riyāʾ as performing acts for the sake of being seen — hidden shirk.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: showing good deeds; Metaphorical: seeking reputation.\n- Root: نفس (nafs)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: self, soul, spirit.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: tazkiyat al-nafs as self-praise and self-purification — forbidden by God.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: self; Metaphorical: self-commendation.\n- Root: حقd (ḥaqida)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to bear malice, to harbor resentment.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-ḥiqd as nursing enmity in the heart — negated from the believer.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: bearing grudge; Metaphorical: hidden rancor.\n- Root: نفاق (nifāq)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: hypocrisy, showing contrary to what one conceals.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: hypocrisy in deeds — outward display contrary to inward state.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: hypocrisy in faith; Metaphorical: hypocrisy in character.\n- Root: علم (ʿalima)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to know, to perceive.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: knowledge as apprehension of reality — its pursuit for other than God is destructive.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: knowledge; Metaphorical: the dangerous burden of the scholar.\n- Root: جدل (jadala)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to argue, to dispute.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-jidāl as contention; al-mirāʾ as disputation with falsehood — condemned.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: argumentation; Metaphorical: obstinate disputation.\n- Root: شرك (sharaka)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to associate, to share.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-riyāʾ as hidden shirk — associating others with God in intention.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: polytheism; Metaphorical: hidden association in deeds.\n- Root: موت (māta)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to die.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: death as separation of soul from body; spiritual death as ruin of the soul.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical death; Metaphorical: spiritual destruction.\n- Root: حيى (ḥayiya)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to live.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: life as the power of perception; eternal life as the reward of the righteous.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical life; Metaphorical: spiritual vitality.\n- Root: هلك (halaka)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to perish, to be destroyed.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-halāk as ruin; eternal destruction as everlasting punishment.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: destruction; Metaphorical: eternal ruin.\n- Root: رياء (riyāʾ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: showing off, ostentation.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: riyāʾ as the incurable disease calling to the greatest sins.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: ostentation; Metaphorical: spiritual disease.\n- Root: جاه (jāh)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: rank, status, prestige.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: love of status (ḥubb al-jāh) among the destructive traits.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: position; Metaphorical: seeking prestige.\n- Root: مباهاة (mubāhāh)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: mutual boasting, vying in splendor.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: mubāhāh as each seeking to display superiority over the other.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: mutual boasting; Metaphorical: rivalry in honor.\n- Root: مماراة (mumārāh)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: disputation, wrangling.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-mirāʾ as contentious argument — condemned by the Prophet.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: disputation; Metaphorical: obstinate wrangling.\n- Root: فخر (fakhara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to boast, to pride oneself.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-fakhr as self-glorification — among the blameworthy traits.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: boasting; Metaphorical: vainglory.\n- Root: عجب (ʿajiba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to wonder, to be amazed.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-ʿujb as self-admiration — among the destructive traits.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: wonder; Metaphorical: self-conceit.\n- Root: بغض (baghiḍa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to hate, to detest.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-baghdāʾ as mutual hatred — contrary to brotherhood.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: hatred; Metaphorical: mutual enmity.\n- Root: غضب (ghaḍiba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to be angry.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-ghaḍab as anger — among the blameworthy traits.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: anger; Metaphorical: rage.\n- Root: طمع (ṭamiʿa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to covet, to desire.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-ṭamaʿ as covetousness — among the destructive traits.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: desire; Metaphorical: greed.\n- Root: أشر (ashira)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to be exultant, to be insolent.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-ashar as excessive joy and insolence — blameworthy.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: exultation; Metaphorical: insolence.\n- Root: بطر (baṭira)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to be arrogant, to be ungrateful.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-baṭar as arrogance in prosperity — among the destructive traits.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: arrogance; Metaphorical: ingratitude for blessings.\n- Root: خيلاء (khuyalāʾ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: pride, haughtiness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-khuyalāʾ as pride and conceit — blameworthy.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: pride; Metaphorical: haughty bearing.\n- Root: خشية (khashyah)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: fear, awe.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-khashyah as reverential fear — among the traits of the believers.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: fear; Metaphorical: reverential awe.\n- Root: رحمة (raḥmah)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: mercy, compassion.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-raḥmah as tenderness of heart — among the traits of the believers.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: mercy; Metaphorical: compassion.\n- Root: غفلة (ghaflah)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: heedlessness, inattention.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-ghaflah as heedlessness — contrary to vigilance.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: inattention; Metaphorical: spiritual heedlessness.\n- Root: خشوع (khushūʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: humility, submissiveness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-khushūʿ as humility — among the traits of the believers.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: humility; Metaphorical: devotional submission.\n- Root: تذكير (tadhkīr)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: reminding, admonishing.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-tadhkīr as reminding of God — a righteous act.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: reminding; Metaphorical: preaching.\n- Root: وعظ (waʿaẓa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to admonish, to exhort.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-waʿẓ as exhortation to obedience and warning against disobedience.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: admonition; Metaphorical: sermonizing.\n- Root: فتوى (fatwā)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: legal ruling, formal opinion.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-fatwā as clarification of legal rulings.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: legal opinion; Metaphorical: religious authority.\n- Root: قضاء (qaḍāʾ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: judgment, decree.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-qaḍāʾ as judgment between people.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: judgment; Metaphorical: judicial authority.\n- Root: وقف (waqafa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to stop, to stand.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-waqf as endowment of property for charitable purposes.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: standing; Metaphorical: religious endowment.\n- Root: ثواب (thawāb)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: reward, recompense.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-thawāb as divine recompense for righteous deeds.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: reward; Metaphorical: divine recompense.\n- Root: عذاب (ʿadhāb)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: punishment, torment.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-ʿadhāb as divine punishment for the disobedient.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: punishment; Metaphorical: divine chastisement.\n- Root: ملك (malaka)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to possess, to rule.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-mulk as sovereignty; al-mulk al-muʾabbad as eternal sovereignty in Paradise.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: dominion; Metaphorical: eternal kingdom.\n- Root: نعيم (naʿīm)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: bliss, delight.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-naʿīm as bliss; al-naʿīm al-sarmad as everlasting bliss.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: delight; Metaphorical: eternal bliss.\n- Root: رخصة (rukhaṣah)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: concession, dispensation.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-rukhaṣah as legal concession.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: concession; Metaphorical: legal dispensation.\n- Root: رياسة (riyāsah)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: leadership, headship.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: love of leadership among the destructive traits.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: leadership; Metaphorical: seeking authority.\n- Root: اندراس (indirās)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: effacement, obliteration.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: indirās al-ʿulūm as the obliteration of the sciences.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: effacement; Metaphorical: extinction of knowledge.\n- Root: صبيان (ṣibyān)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: boys, children.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: children's motivation for learning through play.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: boys; Metaphorical: pupils.\n- Root: مكتب (maktab)\n  * Lisan / Ayn- Root: مكتب (maktab)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: place of instruction, school for boys.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: the elementary school where children are taught.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: school; Metaphorical: place of elementary learning.\n- Root: خلاق (khilāq)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: portion, share, lot.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: \"no portion\" (lā khilāq) in the Hereafter — utter deprivation of divine reward.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: share; Metaphorical: spiritual allotment in the afterlife.\n- Root: فاجر (fājir)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: wicked, immoral, profligate.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: the dissolute one who commits sins openly.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: sinner; Metaphorical: one through whom God nonetheless supports religion.\n- Root: شمع (shamʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: candle, wax taper.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: the candle as parable — consuming itself while giving light to others.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: candle; Metaphorical: one who destroys himself but benefits others.\n- Root: نار (nār)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: fire.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: fire as parable — consuming itself and all around it.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: fire; Metaphorical: one who destroys himself and others.\n- Root: سعد (saʿida)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to be fortunate, to prosper.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: saʿādah as felicity — success in the Hereafter.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: good fortune; Metaphorical: eternal felicity.\n- Root: شقي (shaqiya)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to be wretched, to be miserable.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: shaqāwah as wretchedness — ruin in the Hereafter.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: misery; Metaphorical: eternal ruin.\n- Root: أخلص (akhlaṣa)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to purify, to be sincere.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: ikhlāṣ as purifying intention for God alone — the condition of acceptance.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: sincerity; Metaphorical: pure devotion.\n- Root: ريب (rāba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: to doubt, to suspect.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Theological nuance: al-rayb as doubt and uncertainty — to be dispelled by evidence.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: doubt; Metaphorical: skepticism.", "book_title": "Book of Knowledge", "section_title_ar": "Section 39", "section_title_en": "Section 39", "section_index": 39, "anchors": "*(Anchors established)*", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Book of Knowledge'", "badge": "Insight"}], "36": [{"arabic": "عليه شاهدين إبراهيم وموسى ثم تلا أم لم ينبأ بما في صحف موسى وإبراهيم الذي وفى أن لا تزر وازرة وزر أخرى فقال زياد خلوا سبيله هذا رجل قد لقن حجته وقيل مكتوب في الإنجيل من استغفر لمن ظلمه فقد هزم الشيطان فضيلة الرفق اعلم أن الرفق محمود ويضاده العنف والحدة والعنف نتيجة الغضب والفظاظة والرفق واللين نتيجة حسن", "english": "A man was brought to Hishām concerning whom word had reached him. When he was made to stand before him, he began to speak in his own defense. Hishām said to him: You speak as well? The man said: O Commander of the Faithful, Allah, Mighty and Majestic, said: {«يَوْمَ تَأْتِي كُلُّ نَفْسٍ تُجَادِلُ عَنْ نَفْسِهَا»} — \"The Day when every soul will come disputing on its own behalf.\" Shall we dispute before Allah the Exalted, and not speak a word before you? Hishām said: Yes, woe to you — speak.", "book_title": "Condemnation of Envy", "section_title_ar": "Section 715", "section_title_en": "** Section 715 — On the Virtue of Forbearance (al-Ḥilm) and Gentleness (al-Rifq)", "section_index": 715, "anchors": "**\n\n- **Root: عفو (ʿ-F-W)**\n  - *Lisan / Ayn:* Al-ʿAfw is the effacing and obliteration of the trace of a thing; to pardon is to efface the trace of the offense entirely.\n  - *Al-Raghib (Mufradat):* Al-ʿAfw is the erasure of the sin such that no consequence remains — it is the highest form of forgiveness, surpassing al-ghufrān which veils but does not necessarily efface.\n  - *Al-Zamakhshari (Asas):* Literal: \"عفا الأثر\" — the trace was effaced. Metaphorical: pardon as the effacing of the offense's consequence, as in \"عفا عن ذنبه.\"\n\n- **Root: حلم (Ḥ-L-M)**\n  - *Lisan / Ayn:* Al-Ḥilm is forbearance, clemency, and the restraint of the soul from agitation upon provocation.\n  - *Al-Raghib (Mufradat):* Al-Ḥilm is the soul's composure and its withholding from haste; it is a virtue opposed to al-ṭaysh (levity) and al-jahl (foolishness) — not ignorance, but the deliberate restraint of passion.\n  - *Al-Zamakhshari (Asas):* Literal: dreams (aḥlām) share the root — the state of being unmoved. Metaphorical: the forbearing man (ḥalīm) whose soul does not stir toward vengeance when provoked.\n\n- **Root: رفق (R-F-Q)**\n  - *Lisan / Ayn:* Al-Rifq is gentleness, lenity, and the opposite of violence (al-ʿunf).\n  - *Al-Raghib (Mufradat):* Al-Rifq is the disposition toward ease and gentleness in all affairs; it is praised by the Law and is a quality of the prophets.\n  - *Al-Zamakhshari (Asas):* Literal: \"رفق به\" — he was gentle with him. Metaphorical: gentleness in conduct as a mode of spiritual excellence.\n\n- **Root: صفح (Ṣ-F-Ḥ)**\n  - *Lisan / Ayn:* Al-Ṣafḥ is turning away from an offense and meeting it with a generous countenance; from ṣafḥah (cheek/face) — to turn one's face away from the wrong.\n  - *Al-Raghib (Mufradat):* Al-Ṣafḥ is the overlooking of the offense combined with graciousness — it is more than mere pardon, for it includes magnanimity (al-Ifḍāl).\n  - *Al-Zamakhshari (Asas):* Literal: \"صفح عنه\" — he turned his face away from him. Metaphorical: to turn away from requital and instead bestow favor.\n\n- **Sibawayh Rule:** *Bāb al-Ḥaṣr wa al-Taqyīd bi-innamā* (Restriction & Focused Predication) — \"إنما أعطى ما أعطى بأربع خصال\" employs innamā to restrict the cause of the gift to four qualities, and \"ليس الحليم من ظلم فحلم حتى إذا قدر انتقم\" uses laysa to negate the predicate entirely before affirming the true definition.\n\n---\n\n**", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Condemnation of Envy'", "badge": "Insight"}], "39": [{"arabic": "إلا بعمل صالح فارجعنا نعمل صالحا فقد علمنا الآن صدقك في قولك وأن ليس للإنسان إلا ما سعى وأن سعيه سوف يرى كلما ألقى فيها فوج سألهم خزنتها ألم يأتكم نذير قالوا بلى قد جاءنا نذير أي ألم نسمعكم سنة الله في عباده وأنه", "english": "I say: God—exalted is He—loves the obedient and hates the disobedient. So just as He does not hate the obedient father because of His hatred for the disobedient son, likewise He does not love the disobedient son because of His love for the obedient father. And if love were to flow from the father to the son, then hatred would likewise be bound to flow. Rather, the truth is: {«ولا تزر وازرة وزر أخرى»} — \"And no bearer of burdens will bear the burden of another\" {Quran 6:164, 17:15, 35:18, 39:7, 53:38}. And whoever supposes that he will be saved by his father's piety is like one who supposes he will be satiated by his father's eating, quenched by his father's drinking, become a scholar by his father's learning, and reach the Ka'bah and see it by his father's walking.", "book_title": "Patience and Gratitude", "section_title_ar": "Section 889", "section_title_en": "The Reality of Hope (Al-Rajā') and the Futility of Reliance on Another's Piety", "section_index": 889, "anchors": "- Root: و ل د (walada)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Physical begetting and offspring; the father's generative act.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الولد: المولود\" — offspring as a created entity; here used metaphorically for spiritual causation.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical birth. Metaphorical: \"تولدت العصبية\" — something generated or produced from another; Ghazali exploits this to refute the notion that a father's obedience \"generates\" salvation for the son.\n- Root: ر ج و (rajā)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Expectation, hope, longing for a favorable outcome.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Hope tied to legitimate means; not mere wishful thinking (tamannī).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: awaiting rain. Metaphorical: \"رجوت فلانا\" — to expect from someone; Ghazali contrasts true hope (with action) vs. delusion (ghurūr).\n- Root: غ ر ر (gharara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Deception, heedlessness, being lured by a false appearance.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The state of one who is duped by Satan's plausible speech.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: a risky venture. Metaphorical: \"اغتر بفلان\" — to be deceived by outward charm while the inner reality is corrupt.\n- Root: ك ي س (kays)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Intelligence, shrewdness, prudence.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The wise man who disciplines his soul and acts for the afterlife.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: alertness. Metaphorical: \"الكيس من دان نفسه\" — the prudent one who holds his soul to account; antonym of \"الأحمق\" (the fool).\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — \"إنما يوفى الصابرون أجرهم بغير حساب\" style; Ghazali uses \"إنما\" to restrict reward to personal action, and \"ليس للإنسان إلا ما سعى\" as a restrictive negative clause (nafy + illā) to deny any transfer of merit.", "content_type": "general", "topic": "Classical commentary from Al-Ghazali's Ihya Ulum al-Din, from 'Patience and Gratitude'", "badge": "Commentary"}], "59": [{"arabic": "واحتجوا بقوله تعالى أفمن هذا الحديث تعجبون وتضحكون ولا تبكون وأنتم سامدون قال ابن عباس رضي الله عنهما هو الغناء بلغة حمير يعني السمد فنقول ينبغي أن يحرم الضحك وعدم البكاء أيضا لآن الآية تشتمل عليه فإن قيل إن ذلك مخصوص بالضحك على المسلمين لإسلامهم فهذا أيضا مخصوص بأشعارهم وغنائهم في معرض الاستهزاء بالمسلمين كما قال تعالى والشعراء يتبعهم الغاوون وأراد به شعراء الكفار ولم يدل ذلك على تحريم نظم الشعر في نفسه واحتجوا بما روى جابر رضي الله عنه أنه صلى الله عليه وسلم قال كان إبليس أول من ناح وأول من تغنى (1) فقد جمع بين النيا", "english": "And they argued with His saying—exalted is He: {«أَفَمِنْ هَٰذَا الْحَدِيثِ تَعْجَبُونَ وَتَضْحَكُونَ وَلَا تَبْكُونَ وَأَنتُمْ سَامِدُونَ»} (Do you then wonder at this statement, and laugh, and do not weep, while you amuse yourselves proudly? Q 53:59-61). Ibn 'Abbas—may God be pleased with him—said: \"It (al-sumūd) is singing in the language of Himyar.\" So we say: then laughter and the absence of weeping should also be declared unlawful, because the verse encompasses them. If it is said: \"That is specifically about laughing at the Muslims because of their Islam,\" then this [verse they cite] is also specifically about their poetry and their singing in the manner of mocking the Muslims, just as He said—exalted is He: {«وَالشُّعَرَاءُ يَتَّبِعُهُمُ الْغَاوُونَ»} (And the poets — the deviators follow them; Q 26:224), intending thereby the poets of the disbelievers. And that did not indicate the unlawfulness of composing poetry in itself.", "book_title": "Fear and Hope", "section_title_ar": "Section 499", "section_title_en": "On the Distinction Between Blameworthy and Permissible Singing, and the Refutation of Those Who Declare All Singing Unlawful", "section_index": 499, "anchors": "- Root: حرم (ḥ-r-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of prohibition and sanctity; that which is rendered forbidden either by divine decree, legal ruling, or coercive force.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الحرام: الممنوع منه إما بتسخير إلهي وإما بشري\" — prohibition is either by divine constraint, legal prescription, or rational necessity; the term carries degrees of restriction.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): that which is legally forbidden. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"جلد محرم: لم يدبغ\" — untanned hide; \"أعرابي محرم: حاف لم يخالط الحضر\" — a Bedouin who has not mixed with settled life. Thus, what is \"unlawful\" in the absolute sense must be distinguished from what is merely deficient or uncultivated.\n- Root: ضحك (ḍ-ḥ-k)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The physical expansion of the face and baring of teeth from joy of the soul; the front teeth are called \"الضواحك\" (the smiling ones).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الضحك: انبساط الوجه وتكشر الأسنان من سرور النفس\" — laughter is the expansion of the face from inner joy; \"واستعير الضحك للسخرية\" — metaphorically extended to mockery and derision.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: genuine joy and amusement. Metaphorical: \"ضحكت الأرض عن النبات\" — the earth \"laughs\" forth vegetation; \"ضحك العارض: برق\" — the cloud \"laughs\" with lightning. Thus, the Qur'anic condemnation of laughter (in Q 53:59-60) is contextually bound to mockery of revelation, not laughter per se.\n- Root: روي (r-w-y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To drink one's fill; satiation with water.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"ماء رواء، وروى، أي: كثير مرو\" — abundant water that quenches; extended metaphorically to satiation with knowledge or beauty.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: quenching of thirst. Metaphorical: \"هو ريان من العلم\" — saturated with knowledge; \"سحاب روي: عظيم القطر\" — a cloud heavy with rain. The root carries the sense of fullness and completion, relevant to the \"full\" transmission of reports.\n- Root: ثني (th-n-y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To fold, double, or repeat; the second of a pair.\n  * Al-Raghib (Al-Mufradat): \"الثني والاثنان أصل لمتصرفات هذه الكلمة\" — the root of duality and repetition; \"الثنى: ما يعاد مرتين\" — that which is repeated twice.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: folding one thing upon another. Metaphorical: \"ثنيت فلانا على وجهه إذا رجعته إلى حيث جاء\" — to turn someone back; \"ثنى عنانه عني\" — to turn away one's reins, i.e., to avert. This root governs the \"Thaniyyat al-Wada'\" (the mountain passes of farewell) in the famous song cited in the text.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction and Specification) — When a general prohibition (like \"all singing is unlawful\") is advanced, the specification of particular instances (like the singing of the two girls on the day of Eid) restricts the general claim. Conversely, when a specific permission is established by an unambiguous act (fi'l), it cannot be overridden by ambiguous reports (aqwal) that admit of interpretation and specification.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Fear and Hope'", "badge": "Hadith"}]}, "wordTiming": {"minshawi": {"1": [[1, 200, 1531], [2, 1531, 2337], [3, 2337, 3540]], "53:1": [[1, 200, 1531], [2, 1531, 2337], [3, 2337, 3540]], "2": [[1, 200, 738], [2, 738, 1174], [3, 1174, 2344], [4, 2344, 3230], [5, 3230, 4350]], "53:2": [[1, 200, 738], [2, 738, 1174], [3, 1174, 2344], [4, 2344, 3230], [5, 3230, 4350]], "3": [[1, 200, 1066], [2, 1066, 1897], [3, 1897, 2217], [4, 2217, 3900]], "53:3": [[1, 200, 1066], [2, 1066, 1897], [3, 1897, 2217], [4, 2217, 3900]], "4": [[1, 200, 565], [2, 565, 1112], [3, 1112, 2048], [4, 2048, 2893], [5, 2893, 4560]], "53:4": [[1, 200, 565], [2, 565, 1112], [3, 1112, 2048], [4, 2048, 2893], [5, 2893, 4560]], "5": [